WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.720
Two hindbrain segments look almost alike.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.640
One builds the neurons that work the jaw.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.280
Another builds those that move the face.

4
00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.140
What tells them apart?

5
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:18.153
From above, the early hindbrain is a row of bulges called rhombomeres.

6
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:21.873
They run from r1 to r7, front to back.

7
00:00:22.867 --> 00:00:29.027
In 1989, Andrew Lumsden and Roger Keynes mapped where these motor neurons arise, in

8
00:00:29.027 --> 00:00:30.027
chick embryos.

9
00:00:30.867 --> 00:00:35.087
Trigeminal neurons, for jaw muscles, came from r2 and r3.

10
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:38.307
Facial neurons came from r4 and r5.

11
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:42.277
Swallowing neurons came from r6 and r7.

12
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:44.980
Are the bulges real units?

13
00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:50.160
Label one cell before boundaries appear, and one after.

14
00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:52.260
Where do their descendants go?

15
00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:03.323
In 1990, Scott Fraser, Keynes and Lumsden did it with a tracer dye.

16
00:01:04.343 --> 00:01:06.663
Clones labelled early could span two rhombomeres.

17
00:01:07.623 --> 00:01:10.883
Clones labelled after boundaries formed stayed inside one.

18
00:01:11.867 --> 00:01:16.487
Each rhombomere is a compartment: its progenitors do not cross into the next.

19
00:01:17.307 --> 00:01:19.487
Some neurons born later still migrate across.

20
00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:28.890
Units that keep their cells need a label: a set of transcription factors, mostly

21
00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:29.890
from Hox genes.

22
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:34.513
In mouse, Hox domains have front edges at rhombomere boundaries.

23
00:01:35.533 --> 00:01:36.853
Read the code column by column.

24
00:01:37.773 --> 00:01:38.833
Every rhombomere differs.

25
00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:44.293
Rhombomeres r3 and r4 share Hoxa2 and Hoxb2.

26
00:01:44.973 --> 00:01:47.413
What separates them is Krox20 in r3.

27
00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:50.433
And later, strong Hoxb1 in r4.

28
00:01:51.467 --> 00:01:57.247
One idea to carry through: more early retinoic acid, a more posterior code; the code

29
00:01:57.247 --> 00:01:58.247
helps set the neurons.

30
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:04.750
Early in the hindbrain, more retinoic acid, a vitamin A product, pushes a segment

31
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.510
toward a more posterior identity.

32
00:02:07.583 --> 00:02:13.103
That combination, sharpened by segment genes such as Krox20, helps decide which

33
00:02:13.103 --> 00:02:14.243
motor neurons it builds.

34
00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:20.843
So lookalike segments are not alike.

35
00:02:21.703 --> 00:02:22.843
Each carries its own code.
